I've been messing around with "Viperian" and AstroBASIC today.  Though the game is slow and it is irritating me, it's pretty fun to look through its BASIC listing.  I haven't played around with BASIC in quite some time.  I forgot that AstroBASIC programmers used tricks to get around shortcoming, and sometimes the lines are a bit "knoty," like this one:


136 box A,B,5,5,C;

    if ABS(X)<4

       if ABS(Y)<4

          for W=1to 2;

             for Z=36to 12step -1;

                MO=Z;

             next Z;

          next W;

          S=S+2;

          G=8;

          Q=4;

          BC=8;

          gosub 6

There is no need to explain this line to me.  It is just fun to break it down so that it looks a little easier to understand.

I also tried the trace (the blue and gold buttons held down together at the same time), which is useful, I suppose, but it's hard to use in a game.  Too bad the output  of trace can't be directed to an external monitor.

@Kevin Bunch: Have you tried "Viperian" on a real Astrocade yet?  Maybe you can have a short, five-minute show about it being archived and include some footage of it.  It's a very good example of a BASIC game that uses no machine language routines at all.

Adam

21452:
I have taken up Ken Lill's small challenge that he made on May 7th about "Cruncher" when he wrote:

"now for 1 or more of your readers to figure out what is happening in the program. I'm curious to see if anyone has the guts to figure it out, what all the variables are for and just what they do.

It's doubtful that I really have the "guts" to follow a deep look into "Cruncher," but I have begun this process, if only slowly for now.  So far, I've only reformatted the program and made some hand-written notes for myself that list the places where there are GOSUBs, GOTOs and FOR loops.  I'm happy to report that every FOR loop has a closing NEXT statement, but sometimes they're not nested properly, meaning that they seem out of order due to the use of GOTO commands.  Perhaps this is how BASIC programs originally got the nickname of "spaghetti code."

I'm okay with this continuous jumping around in the "Cruncher" program.  I'm rather sure that without using programming techniques like those employed by most Bally BASIC programmers that no quality games could have been created in the 1.8K of RAM that frugal programmers had to use.

I have attached my reformatted listing of "Cruncher" as a RTF document.
 Hopefully most people can open it in their word processor of choice.  It is called:

Cruncher_(1982)(The_Tiny_Arcade)(2000 baud)(BASIC_Listing)(Reformatted).rtf

I'm also inserting the BASIC listing here directly into this post, but I'm pretty sure that the formatting will be ruined.  If it looks garbled, then please see the attached version of the "Cruncher" AstroBASIC listing.

Adam

